1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 { The Evans gambit essentially belongs to the 19th century, but White players still try it occasionally, hoping to surprise Black, so it is important to be prepared. I found a great way to obtain a promising position without having to remember a lot of lines. -KEY- } Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. O-O { This slow continuation gives Black time to comfortably develop his forces. } d6 7. Qb3 Nh6 $1 { We need to finish the development as fast as possible. We won't be afraid of any Bxh6 because White won't be able to make use of those weaknesses while he'd part with his bishop pair. } ( { Also note that } 7... Qe7 8. d4 { is a bit problematic once we played ...d6 because d5 followed by Qa4 with a check is a threat. } ) 
8. d4 O-O 9. Bg5 Qe8 10. Nbd2 Kh8 $15 { Preparing ...f5 or ...Ng8-e7. White will have a hard time trying to demonstrate full compensation for the pawn. } 
