1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 { The Evans gambit essentially belongs to the 19th century, but White players still try it occasionally, hoping to surprise Black, so it is important to be prepared. I found a great way to obtain a promising position without having to remember a lot of lines. } Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. O-O { This slow continuation gives Black time to comfortably develop his forces. } d6 7. d4 { -KEY- } Bg4 ( 7... Nf6 { is less clear because } 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 dxe5 10. Qb3 O-O 11. Ba3 { wins the exchange. } ) 
8. Qa4 $2 { This activity is just over optimistic. } Bxf3 9. gxf3 exd4 10. cxd4 a6 $1 { A key move preparing a crucial offensive and defensive idea of ...b5 } 11. Bd5 Ne7 12. Bxc6+ Nxc6 13. d5 b5 { Without this move we would be in trouble. } 14. Qa3 Nd4 15. Qxa5 { White still won a piece but now Black develops an irresistible attack. } Qf6 16. Qa3 ( { This was played in Chigorin -Steinitz, World Championship 1892 but now Black erred with } 16. Qa3 Nc2 { - then } 17. Qd3 Nxa1 18. Qe2 { is great for White because the knight on a1 will be lost. Instead, he is winning after: } ) 
b4 $1 17. Qb2 { This saves a Rook on a1 but loses the game in a different way. } ( 17. Qxb4 Nxf3 { is mate in 8 } { _ } ) 
Nxf3+ 18. Kg2 Nh4+ 19. Kh3 Qf3+ 20. Kxh4 Qg2 $19 { Black either wins the rook on f1 or mates White. One way or the other the king on h4 will not be able to survive long. } 
