1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 { The Evans gambit essentially belongs to the 19th century, but White players still try it occasionally, hoping to surprise Black, so it is important to be prepared. I found a great way to obtain a promising position without having to remember a lot of lines. } Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. Qb3 $2 { In this position it's just a one move threat and as the analysis proves, Black gets a big advantage. -KEY- } Qe7 ( { Here } 6... Nh6 { is less accurate because without ...d6 the e5 pawn might be hanging. For example } 7. d4 O-O 8. d5 ) 
7. d4 Nf6 8. Ba3 { This was suggested by Jan Pinski in 'Italian Game and Evans Gambit' (Everymanchess 2005) but it doesn't help White. } d6 9. d5 Nd4 10. Qa4+ ( { Pinski suggested } 10. Nxd4 exd4 11. Qa4 { but he didn't mention } Bd7 { after which } 12. Qxa5 Qxe4 13. Kd1 O-O { is completely winning for Black. Still, you may want to have a look with the engine but it's around -4. } ) 
Bd7 11. Qxa5 Nc2+ 12. Kd1 Nxa1 13. Nbd2 Ng4 $1 { Black starts active kingside play before White takes the knight. } 14. Rf1 f5 $17 { Black has excellent and easy play. } 
