1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 { The Evans gambit essentially belongs to the 19th century, but White players still try it occasionally, hoping to surprise Black, so it is important to be prepared. I found a great way to obtain a promising position without having to remember a lot of lines. } Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 { -KEY- } b5 $5 { I believe this is a great practical choice. Black not only effortlessly achieves at least an equality, he also has a chance of throwing White completely off preparation, due to the rareness of this move. All that at a cost of returning the sacrificed pawn, which Black does in many other lines anyway. } 7. Bd5 { This doesn't pose any problems. Black just plays natural moves. } Nge7 8. dxe5 O-O 9. O-O Rb8 { Black is fully prepared for action. White should be careful not to end up worse, as his structure is shattered and the b1 knight still needs to be developed. The position has never been played, so I will just give an exemplary continuation: } 10. Ba3 b4 11. cxb4 Bxb4 12. Bxb4 Nxb4 13. Nc3 Ba6 14. Re1 Ng6 $11 { Black is comfortably equal. } 
